00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.990 align:start position:0%
developer tango gameworks is best known  for horror games and that might skew  perceptions of its upcoming title  ghostwire tokyo after all both its  previous games the evil within and the  evil within two fall squarely into the  survival horror genre with the first  directed by studio founder shinji mikami  the creator of resident evil but while  ghostwire tokyo was described as  spooky by former creative director kumi  nakamura on stage during e3 2019 when it  was first announced ghostwire tokyo  doesn't seem like a game that's likely  to scare you instead it seems like an  indication that tango game works is  venturing into different areas than what  players might expect a more accurate  description for ghostwire tokyo might be  a first-person shooter with ghosts  compared to tango's previous games it's  far more action oriented while elements  of the supernatural are woven all  through its deserted version of tokyo  the tone isn't one that'll engender fear  in fact the moment moment-to-moment  action of ghostwire tokyo looks like  it'll make you feel pretty capable of  taking on vicious strange and unnerving  spectral foes despite their sometimes  frightening appearances gamespot  recently got to watch about 45 minutes  of ghostwire tokyo in action in a  hands-off preview provided by tango  gameworks the portion of the game we saw  was pretty close to the beginning giving  some tutorial-esque looks at how you'll  explore the game's open world tokyo how  you'll interact with certain features to  increase your elemental powers and what  exactly is going on in its strange story  you play as akito a japanese man trapped  in the shibuya section of tokyo during  what is apparently an attack by a group  of masked cultists the event sees a fog  ripped through the area anyone caught in  it vanishes their bodies disappearing  akito survives this fate thanks to a  spirit named kk a man who was hunting  the cultists before he was apparently  killed somehow kk and akito fuse  together and this fusion protects akito  from the cultist's attacks as well as  allows him to access a supernatural  power called ethereal weaving  that power then allows aikido to draw on  elemental energy firing blasts of power  at phantoms and demons to destroy them  the portion of the game we saw was soon  after the fog had swept through shibuya  with kk guiding akito through the city  to find out what happened and discover a  way to stop the cult leader hanaya  before he died kk and his colleagues had  been hunting for and attempting to stop  the cultists for akito the situation is  personal as he's trying to reach his  sister who has seemingly been threatened  by hanaya and the cult from what we  could glean of the story the demo we  watched picked up soon after akito and  kk first encountered one another with  akito getting the hang of ethereal  weaving for the first time while making  his way through the suddenly vacant city  while tokyo is devoid of life it is full  of strangers or various kinds of ghosts  and phantoms as in most open world games  it looks as though you'll be able to  either fight your way through the  spectres or attempt to avoid them as you  travel through tokyo fighting them  brings on the first-person shooter feel  of the game in which aikido uses kk's  power to fling bursts of elemental  energy at enemies those blasts of energy  damage the strangers until akito is able  to reveal a core within them which he  can then apparently lasso with his  spectral powers and yank out of the  ghosts dispelling them you'll keep your  distance against enemies most of the  time but the creatures we saw in the  short demo consistently worked to close  the gap on aquito to get close enough to  slash him with melee attacks ethereal  weaving helps in this regard too  allowing you to call up an energy shield  to parry the blow and bounce the  stranger back as it attacks parrying is  all about timing and it appears that  much of combat will be about crowd  control knocking enemies back with your  attacks and your shield to keep them  busy until you can damage them enough to  get at their cores the good news is if  you can expose the cores of more than  one enemy at a time you can rip them all  free with the same lasso ghostwire tokyo  is an open world game which means it's  fairly open-ended even as kk guides you  through the main quest though aikido  survived the supernatural fog it lingers  throughout the city at the start of the  game so part of what you'll need to do  as you move around is find tori gates  that have been infested by the cult's  power and use ethereal weaving to clear  them of enemies and then cleanse them  that pushes back the fog in a given area  exposing new opportunities to explore  as you make your way around you'll find  spirits who linger throughout the city  some of them are being caged by  strangers requiring you to fight the  ghosts to free the people within them  you can then use human-shaped paper  dolls called katashiro to absorb the  freed souls to help them get out of  shibuya it looks like you'll absorb  souls into katashiro before taking them  to phone booths that hide secret  technology apparently set up by kk and  his ghost hunter buddies the phone  booths allow you to transfer the spirits  out of the fog area where kk says they  should return to normal from what we can  tell there are quite a few spirits to  save in tokyo but you'll likely unlock  rewards for doing so  other lingering spirits in tokyo serve  as points of contact for side quests the  main thrust of the demo however saw kk  guiding akito back to the spirit's  former apartment there aikido was able  to gather some information about the  cult while also laying claim to a bow  and arrow that aikido was able to infuse  with his power  but as is demonstrated earlier in the  demo hanaya is on to aikito and kkk and  is deploying cultists to stop them as  you explore kk's apartment the entire  place is suddenly sealed by a spectral  barrier trapping you inside while the  cult tries to use its power to collapse  the building around you that forces  akito to climb higher making his way  through the building and other  apartments to destroy magical barrier  stones that power the force field it's  in this portion of the demo that  ghostwire tokyo feels like it's drawing  from some of the same dna as tango  gameworks the evil within games in fact  ghostwire started its life as the evil  within three before it shifted to become  something separate busting through  apartment doors aikido finds himself  entering shifting impossible spaces as  he enters the living room of one  apartment it reverses placing him on the  ceiling with the furniture above his  head opening a closet he finds the  barrier stone across a distant gap with  only a narrow window revealing it a  precisely fired arrow destroys the  barrier stone and suddenly the closet  reverts to its natural state with the  spirit world beyond vanishing as aikido  makes his way higher he soon finds  himself in twisted apartments with  furniture floating in the air where he  has to climb up piece by piece to reach  a higher room and the final barrier  stone with the barrier dispelled aikido  and kk start hunting for the cultists  who cast the spell making their way back  down to the tokyo streets investigation  also seems like it will be a major part  of how you interact with ghostwire tokyo  with akito using a spectral vision power  that highlights the path the man took as  he left the building after trying to get  you killed the demo ends soon after but  the impression it leaves is that you'll  be searching the city for multiple  cultists stopping them and whittling  down their numbers before ultimately  going after hanaya himself while we got  only a small snippet of ghost wire tokyo  what we saw looks like a fascinating mix  of what tango gameworks has become known  for and something wholly new while this  isn't a horror game and it's not a third  person shooter ghostwire does draw on  some of the excellently strange locales  of the evil within and the open world  sensibilities of the evil within two the  combination of real world locales and  supernatural threats looks like it'll  work exceedingly well borrowing shooter  elements players are familiar with and  twisting them to create something fresh  and strange there is a lot about ghost  wire tokyo we don't yet know but our  brief look suggests exploring a  ghost-filled tokyo is going to be a  blast
 

